    <!--
      The platform contracts. Under Core/ and not Platform/ because doc 02's own rule for that
      folder is "platform *implementations* of a Core/ contract", and this assembly is the
      contract: interfaces and a handful of value types, referencing nothing but Vixen.Core,
      Vixen.Core.IO and Vixen.Core.Mathematics. Filing it as an implementation had one concrete
      cost — CheckArchitecture forbids Core/ referencing Platform/, so no Core assembly was
      allowed to say the word "window", which is what kept the whole application host out of
      this folder.
    -->
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Platform/Vixen.Platform.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Platform.Tests/Vixen.Platform.Tests.csproj" />
    <!--
      The application host, and the reason the contracts above had to move first. Its namespace is
      Vixen.App and its package is still Tools/Vixen.App — what changed is that the boot sequence
      and the frame loop are compiled as frame code: AOT- and trim-analyzed, API-baselined,
      documentation-gated, and published by Tools/Vixen.AotProbe, none of which the TOOLING profile
      does. What stayed in Tools/ is the three files that name a backend.
    -->
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.App.Hosting/Vixen.App.Hosting.csproj" />
    <!--
      The blockout mesh kernel (docs/plan/24 § D1). Under Core/ and not Editor/ because it is pure
      arithmetic with no document, no selection and no device — so it belongs under the profile that
      is AOT-compatible, trimmable, packable and API-checked, and because a mesh operation is worth
      having at run time: procedural level generation, destructible geometry and a runtime CSG are
      all things the same code answers and none of them can reach an editor assembly.
      ⚠ It references Vixen.Core.Mathematics and nothing else — in particular not Vixen.Rendering,
      because a geometry kernel that needed the render assembly to describe a triangle would be
      backwards. Vixen.Navigation is the precedent and makes the same choice with its own PolyMesh.
    -->
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Geometry/Vixen.Geometry.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Geometry.Tests/Vixen.Geometry.Tests.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Foliage/Vixen.Foliage.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Foliage.Tests/Vixen.Foliage.Tests.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.Terrain/Vixen.Rendering.Terrain.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.Terrain.Tests/Vixen.Rendering.Terrain.Tests.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Terrain/Vixen.Terrain.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Terrain.Tests/Vixen.Terrain.Tests.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Navigation/Vixen.Navigation.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Navigation.Tests/Vixen.Navigation.Tests.csproj" />
    <!--
      Agent decision-making (docs/plan/37). Under Core/ and emphatically not on the gameplay spine:
      doc 28 had put the three planners in Vixen.Gameplay.Ai on top of Vixen.Gameplay.Combat, and a
      behaviour tree that needs a combat package cannot run a stealth patrol or a shopkeeper. What
      stays behind in Gameplay is threat, aggro, leashing and spawn tables — the part that really is
      game rules — and it will reference this rather than contain it.
    -->
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Ai/Vixen.Ai.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Ai.Tests/Vixen.Ai.Tests.csproj" />
    <!--
      And perception beside it rather than inside it (docs/plan/37 § P3), because it is the half that
      needs the world: Vixen.Engine for where things are and Vixen.Physics for what is between them.
      A game that wants behaviour trees without a physics world links Vixen.Ai and stops.
    -->
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Ai.Perception/Vixen.Ai.Perception.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Ai.Perception.Tests/Vixen.Ai.Perception.Tests.csproj" />
    <!--
      And the nodes that touch the rest of the engine (docs/plan/37 § P4). The widest reference list
      in Core/ — navigation, animation and audio at once — which is exactly why it is its own
      assembly: nothing depends on it, so a game links it only if it wants the nodes.
    -->
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Ai.Nodes/Vixen.Ai.Nodes.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Ai.Nodes.Tests/Vixen.Ai.Nodes.Tests.csproj" />
    <!--
      And the gameplay debugger (docs/plan/37 § P7). Split out for the same reason perception is:
      DebugDraw lives in Vixen.Engine and Vixen.Ai depends on no engine. It sees perception too,
      because § D20 asks for *one* debug surface — the cones are drawn beside the active path — and
      the assembly that draws has to see both halves to do that.
    -->
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Ai.Diagnostics/Vixen.Ai.Diagnostics.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Ai.Diagnostics.Tests/Vixen.Ai.Diagnostics.Tests.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering/Vixen.Rendering.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.Tests/Vixen.Rendering.Tests.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.PostFx/Vixen.Rendering.PostFx.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.PostFx.Tests/Vixen.Rendering.PostFx.Tests.csproj" />

    <!--
      The distance-field bake (docs/plan/19 § L1). Beside the renderer rather than inside it because
      it references no device: it turns triangles into a field on the CPU, which is what lets it be
      checked against closed forms instead of against a picture.
    -->
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.DistanceFields/Vixen.Rendering.DistanceFields.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.DistanceFields.Tests/Vixen.Rendering.DistanceFields.Tests.csproj" />

    <!--
      The irradiance field (docs/plan/19 § L2): bricks, a pool and an indirection grid. Beside the
      distance fields and device-free for the same reason — the storage and sampling half is where
      the seams and the leaks live, and both are arithmetic.
    -->
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.IrradianceFields/Vixen.Rendering.IrradianceFields.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.IrradianceFields.Tests/Vixen.Rendering.IrradianceFields.Tests.csproj" />

    <!--
      The screen probe gather (docs/plan/19 § L3): an octahedral radiance map per probe on a coarse
      screen grid. Device-free like its two siblings above, and for the same reason — the mapping,
      the solid angles and the projection are the halves that can be held against closed forms.
    -->
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.ScreenProbes/Vixen.Rendering.ScreenProbes.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.ScreenProbes.Tests/Vixen.Rendering.ScreenProbes.Tests.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.RayTracing/Vixen.Rendering.RayTracing.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.RayTracing.Tests/Vixen.Rendering.RayTracing.Tests.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.Reflections/Vixen.Rendering.Reflections.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.Reflections.Tests/Vixen.Rendering.Reflections.Tests.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.SurfaceCache/Vixen.Rendering.SurfaceCache.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.SurfaceCache.Tests/Vixen.Rendering.SurfaceCache.Tests.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.VirtualGeometry/Vixen.Rendering.VirtualGeometry.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Rendering.VirtualGeometry.Tests/Vixen.Rendering.VirtualGeometry.Tests.csproj" />

    <!--
      The debug-geometry join, listed here rather than beside Vixen.Engine because it belongs to the
      renderer half of the pair: Vixen.Engine accumulates without a device and this draws. Same
      arrangement, and same reason, as Vixen.Ui.Renderer.
    -->
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Engine.Renderer/Vixen.Engine.Renderer.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Engine.Renderer.Tests/Vixen.Engine.Renderer.Tests.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Animation/Vixen.Animation.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Animation.Tests/Vixen.Animation.Tests.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Vfx/Vixen.Vfx.csproj" />
    <Project Path="Core/Vixen.Vfx.Tests/Vixen.Vfx.Tests.csproj" />
